  you could make you have a higher chance  on the other end to have healthy  beautiful embryos  okay um what made you get into becoming  a fertility  specialist so i was doing  a residency program in obstetrics and  gynecology  when i got introduced to the world of  fertility and  for many reasons i thought it was a  perfect career path for myself  i have my undergraduate degree in  psychology  this is an area that on a daily basis  i'm working  with people's emotional well-being and  luckily for me and my personality i'm  able to be there  for patients i make sure that my team is  also very similar to myself that we are  able to hold people's hands through this  process and give the emotional support  that they need  i also love the science behind what i  did every single year  our field is growing rapidly and it's  also one of the newest  areas of medicine it's been around like  30 years and other areas of medicine  have been around hundreds of years  so it's just technologically advancing  very rapidly  i love the fact that there's science  involved and the research keeps changing  and things keep getting better and  better  um there's a lot of things and i i think  it's like i have the biggest gift in the  world because in my occupation  i change people's lives um i may be able  to help gay and lesbian patients  patients that have had major problems in  their health have  families that they never would have had  in the past and it's just beyond  rewarding  to be able to do that for patients so  i'm very fortunate i  and i also was beyond fortunate to have  gotten accepted into one of the  country's number one fellowship programs  which trained me so well  and now to be a partner in one of the  nation's number one fertility centers as  well  that's awesome um i had one question pop  up from instagram and it was a lesbian  couple  and they said how do how do you decide  which egg to use does that really  problem  it's the my best options are with my  lesbian couples because we have two sets  of eggs two sets of uterus  we have a lot of options for us um  there's many ways so sometimes we have a  lesbian couple coming in and they  say well i'm older and i'm going to go  first so we're going to do  artificial insemination with donors  sperm and hopefully it works  sometimes we do what's called reciprocal  ivf where we get the eggs out of the  body  make it with the sperm and then put the  embryo back in the other partner  so everyone's involved so the egg comes  from one partner  but then the other partner is the  gestational carrier um  and surrogate for the embryo there's a  lot of different options on how lesbian  partners do it there are some partners  in a lesbian relationship who just have  a zero desire to be pregnant and then  there's others  that both of them want to carry a baby  we've had every single scenario is  available  um for that and also it's a big part of  our practice by the way  um lgbt community is a big part of  scrc's practice here  yeah that doesn't surprise me that makes  sense um what about do you also end up  hooking up um  like gay partners with surrogate mothers  yeah absolutely so we have an entire  division at southern california  reproductive center here  where we have coordinators nurses  medical assistants who all they do is  help find you your egg donors  help find you your surrogates coordinate  the entire process  of making embryos then transferring them  to different  surrogates and doing that entire process  for our patients  we even have something that i it's a  very rare thing there's only a few  i think i've heard of in the country but  we have what's called a third party  concierge  so if you hire this person um there is a  nominal fee which  it's i think is the best money ever  spent she will actually help you  find your egg donor help you find your  surrogate she has meetings with you  about the characteristics and everything  that you  like to have and want to have um  so you can kind of have that entire  process available to you  and she does the work and finds you  those people yeah it's amazing  okay uh changing topics slightly again  not really but um  does the iud or the birth control pill  do people who have been on that have a  harder time making eggs or getting  pregnant  that you've seen so we always ask  patients if they use an iud  if it was uncomplicated and they didn't  have a problem or an infection or  anything  very unlikely that it would have  affected the lining of the uterus in any  way negatively  the birth control pill if you're on  birth control for a  really really long time sometimes it  really suppresses the ovary meaning that  it just like reduces  the eggs that are awake and it takes a  little while for the ovary to come back  to life  so what's the either of the hard time  look like i think it  uh i mean i've had patients that were on  birth control like 15 years  20 years since their teenage years and  they just got used to being on it  i think it's a good idea for those  patients to assess their fertility  um and be off of the birth control for  anywhere between a month to  three months before trying to start  fertility but then again if you're like  40 years old i don't know if you need to  be waiting three months to start  yeah okay okay fair enough um  let me see do i have any other questions  for you you just burned through those  i think that's i think that's basically  it um  if people are looking for you online  where can they find you  i think the easiest way to find me is my  instagram and the different social media  channels which are dr shahid dadir  it's dr shaheen gadir it's a verified  page  and probably the easiest way i also have  a podcast that launched called the  fertile life  where we talk about all things fertility  related and lifestyle related which has  been launched about a month and a half  ago and doing incredibly well  and very fun so just like i love talking  here we talk a lot about this stuff and  a lot of things  have to do with uh people's life as well  not  just the fertility aspect of it but  other things that affect the fertility  aspect of it  and you can always call the clinic and  make a consultation and we do consults  for patients around the world and people  are flying in these days even with  everything going on  and getting pregnant and leaving  successfully so we're really proud of  that  that's awesome okay so last question i i  do have a few more it turns out  um so these other aspects that can  affect your fertility  um how does say diet or stress  like what are these other factors people  might want to take into consideration  i just like anything in your life i  think trying to be as healthy as  possible promotes  good outcomes and being very unhealthy  promotes bad outcomes so  i think if you're in your reproductive  years and you care about your fertility  and you care about your health  you should try to be as healthy as  possible you know normal amounts of  well-balanced diet and exercise i think  are important for any reproductive age  woman and man  um i think stress stress is awful and it  affects  everything in your body including your  fertility and trying to learn how to  manage that  in a very complicated world right now is  challenging  but you need to do the best that you can  okay well dr gadir thank you very much  for coming on and i will link  your social media and your podcast below  thank you thank you so much that was  fascinating thank you  i love that interview you asked  excellent questions by the way  thank you thank you very much thank you  for having me on today  [Music]  you
 

